# HMU05 <-> POCO M6 Pro 5G pairing attempt (decisive phone test)

Date: 2026-10-09
Device (HMU05, OpenWrt):  BD 02:00:C2:B9:10:3D, hci0 Bus SMD, Features ff fe 8f fe d8 3f 5b 87
Device agent:              bt-agent -c NoInputNoOutput  (auto-confirm via `yes |`)
Peer (POCO M6 Pro 5G):     BD 9C:9E:D5:52:D0:A3, class 0x5a020c, RSSI -46
Capture:                   device-side `hcidump -x -t` on hci0

## Result: FAIL at SSP DHKey check (status 0x05) — identical to the fedora/BCM4377 host

Key sequence:
  08:50:49.319  > Connect Request              bdaddr 9C:9E:D5:52:D0:A3
  08:50:49.333  > Connect Complete             handle 11 status 0x00
  08:50:50.350  > IO Capability Response       capability 0x01 (DisplayYesNo) auth 0x03 (Dedicated Bonding, MITM)
  08:50:50.351  < IO Capability Request Reply  capability 0x03 (NoInputNoOutput) auth 0x02 (No MITM)  => Just Works
  08:50:51.702  > User Confirmation Request    passkey 282754
  08:50:51.702  < User Confirmation Request Reply  (auto-confirmed)
  08:50:55.579  > Simple Pairing Complete      status 0x05  Error: Authentication Failure   <== FAIL
  08:50:57.648  < Disconnect reason 0x13 (Remote User Terminated Connection)

## Why this is decisive

The POCO M6 Pro 5G is a known-good, independent Android BT host. The fedora/BCM4377
host is a second known-good host. BOTH fail at the exact same HCI event:
`Simple Pairing Complete (0x36) status 0x05 (Authentication Failure)`.

The DHKey check (f3) is computed entirely inside the BR/EDR controller — the host
only relays IO-capability and user-confirmation events. Two unrelated, healthy hosts
producing the same controller-side authentication failure => the fault is in the
HMU05 WCNSS BT controller/firmware, not in the host, the association model, the
MITM setting, or the advertised BD address.

